<!doctype html>
{% comment %}
  ============================================================================
  layout/suf.liquid — the layout for rebuilt pages.
  ============================================================================

  Selected per template, not globally. A JSON template opts in with a top-level
  key; a Liquid template with a tag:

      { "layout": "suf", "sections": { ... } }        <- JSON
      {% layout 'suf' %}                              <- Liquid

  templates/password.json already uses the JSON form for layout/password.liquid,
  which is the proof this works on a Vintage theme.

  IT LOADS suf.css AND suf.js, AND NOTHING ELSE. No inherited CSS or
  JavaScript at all -- roughly 850 KB less than theme.liquid serves on every
  page. Keep it that way: if something new needs a library, port the thing
  that needs it.
{% endcomment %}
<html lang="{{ request.locale.iso_code }}">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <meta name="theme-color" content="{{ settings.primary_color }}">

    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://cdn.shopify.com" crossorigin>

    {%- if canonical_url != blank -%}
      <link rel="canonical" href="{{ canonical_url }}">
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- if settings.favicon != blank -%}
      <link rel="shortcut icon" href="{{ settings.favicon | image_url: width: 32 }}" type="image/png">
    {%- endif -%}

    {% render 'SEO-tags' %}
    {% render 'social-meta-tags' %}

    {% comment %}
      The whole typeface, in one file carrying the 100-900 axis.

      It is preloaded because `font-display: swap` paints in Arial and swaps
      when the font lands, and the preload is what usually gets it there before
      first paint. Nothing here needs revisiting when the design reaches for a
      new weight -- one file already covers every one of them.
    {% endcomment %}
    {{ 'hanken-grotesk-variable.woff2' | asset_url | preload_tag: as: 'font', type: 'font/woff2', crossorigin: 'anonymous' }}


    {{ 'suf.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}

    {% comment %}
      Theme-editor values -> custom properties, after suf.css so they override
      the fallbacks compiled into it.
    {% endcomment %}
    {% render 'suf-tokens' %}

    {% comment %}
      Enables the scroll-in accents. See
      frontend/styles/components/_suf-motion.scss.

      INLINE AND IN THE HEAD. The resting state of an accent is opacity 0, so
      the stylesheet applies it only on a page that can undo it -- a JS failure
      then costs the animation rather than the content. It cannot live in the
      deferred assets/suf.js, which would paint them visible, hide them, then
      animate them back.

      NOT IN THE THEME EDITOR. Selecting or editing a block re-renders its
      section, and the observer in assets/suf-motion.js never sees the replaced
      nodes -- so every accent it would have revealed stays at opacity 0 and a
      merchant edits a block they cannot see. Without the attribute nothing is
      hidden in the first place, which is the same escape hatch a blocked
      script gets.

      One line, and it stays one line.
    {% endcomment %}
    {%- unless request.design_mode -%}
      <script>
        document.documentElement.dataset.sufMotion = '';
      </script>
    {%- endunless -%}

    {% comment %}
      set_weights: false because this layout supplies its own. The snippet's
      `h1, h2, ...` and the base layer's `:where(.suf-body) h1` are BOTH
      (0,0,1) -- :where() adds nothing -- and this renders after suf.css, so
      the tie went to the snippet and held every heading at 700 while the
      stylesheet asked for 800.
    {% endcomment %}
    {% render 'suf-fonts', apply: true, set_weights: false, family: "'Hanken Grotesk', Arial, Helvetica, sans-serif" %}

    {% # theme-check-enable ParserBlockingScript %}


    {% comment %}
      Required. Shopify injects analytics, app embeds and the theme editor
      bridge here. Removing it breaks the editor and app blocks.
    {% endcomment %}
    {{ content_for_header }}
  </head>

  <body class="suf-body template-{{ template | replace: '.', ' ' | truncatewords: 1, '' | handle }}">
    <a class="suf-skip-link" href="#suf-main">Skip to content</a>

    {% comment %}
      The back-to-top target. A real element rather than href="#", which
      scrolls the viewport but leaves focus at the bottom of the page, so the
      next Tab resumes in the footer. tabindex="-1" makes it focusable by the
      fragment jump without adding a tab stop.
    {% endcomment %}
    <span id="suf-top" tabindex="-1"></span>

    {% comment %}
      The reveal threshold, as an element: its HEIGHT is the distance, so the
      number lives once in CSS rather than being duplicated in JS. While it is
      in view, suf-totop.js keeps the button hidden.
    {% endcomment %}
    <span class="suf-totop__sentinel" aria-hidden="true"></span>

    {% comment %}
      The header. layout/theme.liquid renders this same section, so both
      layouts read one settings key: one logo, one menu, no drift.
    {% endcomment %}
    {% section 'suf-nav' %}

    <main id="suf-main" class="suf-main">
      {{ content_for_layout }}
    </main>

    {% section 'suf-footer' %}

    {% comment %}
      Back to top. Hidden until the sentinel above scrolls out of view, which
      assets/suf-totop.js watches; the jump itself is CSS smooth scrolling, not
      script.
    {% endcomment %}
    <a class="suf-totop" href="#suf-top">
      <span class="suf-totop__arrow" aria-hidden="true">&uarr;</span>
      <span class="suf-visually-hidden">Back to top</span>
    </a>


    {% comment %} Net-new SUF scripts. Native ES module; type=module implies defer. {% endcomment %}
    <script src="{{ 'suf.js' | asset_url }}" type="module"></script>
  </body>
</html>
